# Never commit files you did not change

Multiple agents work this repo concurrently in the **same worktree**, sharing one
index. Two real collisions were observed in a single session on 2026-09-10:

1. An uncommitted edit to `apps/web/app/agents/[id]/chat/page.tsx` was clobbered
   mid-session and had to be re-applied.
2. Commit `7debe9c0` ("Stop leaking internal chat status labels to users") swept in
   ten files from a different agent's voice/schema work, because the index already
   held them when `git commit` ran.

Nobody is at fault for a race. Committing files you never touched is avoidable.

## Rules

1. **Never** run `git add -A`, `git add .`, `git commit -a`, or `git commit` with no
   pathspec. The index is shared; it is not a record of your work.
2. Commit with explicit paths every time:
   `git commit -F <msgfile> -- <path1> <path2> ...`
   This commits those paths from the working tree and leaves the rest of the index
   untouched, so a concurrent agent's staging survives.
3. Use `git commit -F <file>` for multi-line messages. On PowerShell, a multi-line
   `-m` string is split into pathspecs and the commit fails confusingly.
4. **Never** `git reset`, `git checkout -- .`, `git stash`, or `git restore` across
   paths you do not own. That silently destroys another agent's uncommitted work.
5. Before editing, `git status --short` and note files already modified. If a file
   you need is already dirty and not yours, say so in your report rather than
   overwriting it or "fixing" its failing test to match your guess at intent.
6. After committing, re-verify your own files landed
   (`git show HEAD:<path>`) — and, if a concurrent commit carried them, report the
   attribution plainly instead of silently accepting the wrong commit message.

## PowerShell path gotcha

`[id]` is a wildcard character class in PowerShell, so
`Select-String -Path "app/agents/[id]/chat/page.tsx"` silently matches nothing and
reads as "the file is missing". Use `-LiteralPath`, or the Grep tool. A false
"my change was reverted" report was traced to exactly this.
